# Typically if you want to ignore some dir with fd you'll also want to ignore it with rg
# Both happens to use gitignore globs for this so that's easy.
# Also be wary, you cannot use absolute paths: they'll be interpreted as relative.


# nix dirs
# These primarily prevent any files which are not actually used in the current
# active system configuration from showing up. It also filters out the many duplicate
# results under different symlink paths that will appear when you follow symlinks
# This makes using `fd` with --follow very convenient and fast while still capturing
# everything youd be interested in 99.99% of the time (it is fast even for the root dir
# if you also filter out /proc/ and /sys/ there)
/nix/store/
/nix/var/log/
/nix/var/nix/profiles
/nix/var/nix/gcroots
**/.nix-profile/
**/.local/state/nix/profiles/
**/.local/state/home-manager/gcroots/
**/.nix-defexpr/
**/.cache/nix

# git dir
.git/

# undo dirs
**/.local/state/nvim/undo/

# xdg dirs
.cache
**/.local/share/Trash/

# Game dirs
**/.steam/
**/.local/share/Steam/
**/.wine
**/.local/share/PrismLauncher/
**/.config/r2modmanPlus-local/

